  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` (time field `timestamp`) has one document per web request:\n- **Who sent it:** `clientip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`, `machine.os`, `agent`\n- **What was asked for:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `referer`, `host`\n- **What came back:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text with a `.keyword` copy) and `bytes`\n- **Other:** `tags`, `memory`, `machine.ram`, `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-fc14850c Web logs overview\"** (id `64786353-9533-4043-80f4-9c6f22123384`). It opens on the last 7 days and has 7 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct `clientip`)\n- Average bytes per response\n- Server errors (5xx), filtered on `response.keyword : 5*`\n- Requests over time by response code (stacked bar chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs\n- Traffic by response code (request count and total bytes)\n\n**3. Rule: \"EFF-fc14850c 5xx spike\"** (id `b5942920-f34b-4b24-bb8b-c656d5326c66`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that:\n- runs every minute;\n- counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes;\n- fires when that count is above 10.\n\nIt has no actions attached, so it raises alerts in Kibana but doesn't send any notifications. Add a connector, such as email or Slack, if you want to be notified.",
